            <ArticleTitle>New treatment options for thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>211-215</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5482/HAMO-16-07-0026</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The thrombotic-thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is an acute, life-threatening disease, characterised by enhanced platelet aggregation, disturbed microcirculation and organ dysfunction. With the currently available treatment (plasma exchange, infusions, corticosteroids) mortality ist still as high as 10-15 %. Recent, pathophysiology-based developments may improve the outcome. The most promising candidates for future treatment of TTP are: rituximab for termination of the autoimmune process, caplacizumab for prevention of platelet-VWF-interaction, and recombinant ADAMTS13 for replacement of the inhibited or missing enzyme.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
